<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wolfram von Eschenbach</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1170 – c. 1220</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wolfram von Eschenbach was a German knight and poet, one of the greatest writers of medieval German literature. His epic Parzival, adapted from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chretien-de-troyes">Chrétien de Troyes</a>, tells the story of the knight's quest for the Grail; he also wrote the unfinished Willehalm and Titurel. Landgrave Hermann I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a> was one of his patrons, and Wagner's opera Parsifal is based on his work.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wolfram-von-eschenbach.jpg" alt="Wolfram von Eschenbach" width="320" height="486" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Meister des Codex Manesse · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Codex_Manesse_149v_Wolfram_von_Eschenbach.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Little is known of Wolfram's life. No historical documents mention him, and his works are the sole evidence. In Parzival he speaks of wir Beier, meaning we <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavarians">Bavarians</a>, and the dialect of his works is East Franconian. On this and on geographical references the present-day Wolframs-Eschenbach near Ansbach has been officially designated his birthplace, but the evidence is circumstantial, and at least four other places in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> are named Eschenbach. His works point to several possible patrons, most reliably Hermann I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>, which suggests he served at a number of courts. He often alludes to his own poverty, and in Parzival he states that he is illiterate, a claim that some scholars treat with scepticism and many have credited.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>He is best known for Parzival, sometimes regarded as the best of the German Arthurian romances. It is based on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chretien-de-troyes">Chrétien de Troyes</a>' unfinished Perceval, le Conte du Graal, and is the first surviving German work to treat the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-grail">Holy Grail</a>, which Wolfram presents as a gemstone. His narrator expresses disdain for Chrétien's version and names as his source a poet from Provence called Kyot.</p><p>He also wrote the fragmentary Titurel and the unfinished Willehalm, both after Parzival. Titurel mentions the death of Hermann I, which dates it after 1217, and tells of the lovers Schionatulander and Sigune. Willehalm, based on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-french">Old French</a> chanson de geste Aliscans, is set against wars between Christians and Saracens and survives in 78 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>. His nine surviving songs, five of them dawn-songs, are regarded as masterpieces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minnesang">Minnesang</a>; no melodies survive.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The 84 surviving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> of Parzival show its popularity over the next two centuries. Many manuscripts of Willehalm include a continuation written in the 1240s by Ulrich von Türheim, and around 1272 a poet named Albrecht, generally presumed to be Albrecht von Scharfenberg, expanded Titurel as the Jüngere Titurel. The modern rediscovery of Wolfram begins with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-jakob-bodmer">Johann Jakob Bodmer</a>'s translation of Parzival in 1753. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-wagner">Richard Wagner</a> drew on it for the libretto of Parsifal, and Wolfram appears as a character in Wagner's Tannhäuser.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wolfram von Eschenbach” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Landgrave Hermann I made the Wartburg a centre of Minnesang, hosting poets such as Walther von der Vogelweide and <b>Wolfram von Eschenbach</b>; this was later remembered as the Wartburg contest of song.</blockquote><a href="/state/landgraviate-of-thuringia">Landgraviate of Thuringia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Wolframs-Eschenbach, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.23,10.73&amp;year=1202" aria-label="Wolframs-Eschenbach on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4797 447 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Wolfram von Eschenbach"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5247 717h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5247 717h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.23,10.73&amp;year=1202">What was here in 1202? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;And shaming alike and honour are his who such doubt shall show, For it standeth in evil contrast with a true man's dauntless might, As one seeth the magpie's plumage, which at one while is black and white.&#8221;</p><footer>Parzival, opening lines &#183; tr. Jessie L. Weston, 1894 &#183; of the man of wavering faith, likened to a magpie's plumage &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/47297/pg47297-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
